= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Bernstein polynomial> is
$$
B_n(f,x)=\sum_{j=0}^n f(j/n)\binom nj x^j(1-x)^{n-j}.
$$
It has degree at most $n$, since cancellation can lower the degree. For $0\le x\le1$, the <Bernstein basis> weights are nonnegative and their sum is $(x+(1-x))^n=1$ by the <binomial theorem>. Consequently
$$
|B_n(f,x)|\le\sum_{j=0}^n |f(j/n)|\binom nj x^j(1-x)^{n-j}\le\|f\|_\infty.
$$
Thus the <Bernstein polynomial> defines a <positive linear operator on continuous functions> satisfying **$\|B_n f\|_\infty\le\|f\|_\infty$**. Since $B_n1=1$, its <operator norm> in the <supremum norm> is exactly one.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $j^{\underline m}=j(j-1)\cdots(j-m+1)$ for the <falling factorial>. At the sampling points, $f_{nm}(j/n)=n^{-m}j^{\underline m}$, which vanishes for $j<m$. For $j\ge m$, cancellation of the factorials gives
$$
j^{\underline m}\binom nj=\frac{n!}{(n-m)!}\binom{n-m}{j-m}=n^{\underline m}\binom{n-m}{j-m}.
$$
Insert this into the <Bernstein polynomial> and set $\ell=j-m$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
B_n(f_{nm},x)&=\frac{n^{\underline m}}{n^m}x^m\sum_{\ell=0}^{n-m}\binom{n-m}{\ell}x^\ell(1-x)^{n-m-\ell}\\
&=\frac{n^{\underline m}}{n^m}x^m=f_{nm}(1)x^m.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <binomial theorem> evaluates the remaining sum. For $m=0$, both sides equal one. This proves the **<Bernstein falling-factorial identity> $B_n(f_{nm},x)=f_{nm}(1)x^m$**.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Fix $m$ and take $n\ge m$. On $[0,1]$, every factor $x-r/n$ and every factor $x$ has absolute value at most one. Replacing the factors successively and applying the <triangle inequality> gives
$$
\|f_{nm}-x^m\|_\infty\le\sum_{r=0}^{m-1}\frac rn=\frac{m(m-1)}{2n},\qquad |f_{nm}(1)-1|\le\frac{m(m-1)}{2n}.
$$
Using the <supremum norm> contraction in (a) and the <Bernstein falling-factorial identity> in (b),
$$
\begin{aligned}
\|B_n(x^m)-x^m\|_\infty
&\le\|B_n(x^m-f_{nm})\|_\infty+\|B_n(f_{nm})-x^m\|_\infty\\
&\le\|x^m-f_{nm}\|_\infty+|f_{nm}(1)-1|\le\frac{m(m-1)}n.
\end{aligned}
$$
For $m=0,1$, the <monomials> are reproduced exactly. For every fixed $m$, **$B_n(x^m)\to x^m$ uniformly on $[0,1]$**.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <Weierstrass approximation theorem> states that the <polynomials> are a <dense subspace> of $C[0,1]$ in the <supremum norm>. By <linearity> and (c), $B_np\to p$ uniformly for every <polynomial> $p$. Given $f\in C[0,1]$ and $\varepsilon>0$, choose a <polynomial> $p$ with $\|f-p\|_\infty<\varepsilon$. The <operator norm> estimate in (a) gives
$$
\|B_nf-f\|_\infty\le\|B_n(f-p)\|_\infty+\|B_np-p\|_\infty+\|p-f\|_\infty\le2\varepsilon+\|B_np-p\|_\infty.
$$
First let $n\to\infty$, then $\varepsilon\downarrow0$. Hence **$\|B_nf-f\|_\infty\to0$ for every continuous $f$**. This is an instance of <extension of approximation operators from a dense subspace>.

